  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Around Le Lavandou and Bormes-les-Mimosas, dog-friendly options are scarce. Bormes stands out as an exception with a stretch of beach tolerated between the boat garages and the Fort de Brégançon, the town's iconic presidential residence. Two consistent sources (Bormes's official tourist office and a regional tourism website) help clarify the rule: on the Fort de Brégançon beach itself, dogs are allowed year-round and can run freely in a dedicated area, provided they are kept on leash on the access path.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>On Cabasson beach, a little further on (Parc Capalo area), dogs are only tolerated from November to March, by contrast — so not year-round, unlike Brégançon. This beach is only reachable on foot, with compulsory paid parking at Parc Capalo. Don't confuse the two: the rule differs noticeably from one beach to the other even though they're neighbours.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the coastal path between Bormes and Le Lavandou offers a nice alternative if the beach isn't well suited on the day of your visit.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>La Londe-les-Maures, just to the west, offers a small dog water-entry spot — a good backup option in the area.</p>
